 :doc/body "# OpenRouter provider application — Murakumo\n\nStatus: DRAFT / NOT SUBMITTED. This replaces the September 1 application narrative, not the independent Basho/Hokusai product roadmap. Do not send until the gates below are evidenced.\n\n## Proposed offering\nProduct concept: Slow, but cheap. Murakumo is a cost-first distributed compute cluster for workloads that can tolerate waiting: document processing, offline generation and other deadline-tolerant jobs. This is a product direction, not evidence that the currently qualified single b70 backend performs multi-node model-parallel inference.\nThe candidate price is USD 0.10 per million input tokens and USD 0.40 per million output tokens. These are proposed catalog prices, not proof of profitability or a lowest-market-price claim. Historical unit-cost estimates for another model cannot validate this dense 27B candidate.\nMurakumo proposes a self-hosted upstream text model, qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70, served through https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1/chat/completions. It is not an original model or a proprietary fine-tune. The advertised alias murakumo-main is unsuitable for this application because its response can identify a different backend. Basho's current upstream relay and the untrained Hokusai artifact are not part of this proposal.\n\nCandidate catalog: https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1/openrouter/models. This separate projection uses the official ModelDocumentV2 schema version 2.4 and deliberately reports is_ready=false. It makes no ZDR, certified-compliance, dedicated-capacity or proprietary-training claim. The existing /v1/models remains the product discovery surface, not the application catalog.\n\n## Evidence as of 2026-09-08\nTwo initial public requests to the fixed candidate returned HTTP 200, the requested model ID, correct changing marker text, stop, and consistent positive prompt/completion usage. The streaming request also supplied [DONE]. These are low-volume protocol checks, not a model-weight attestation, uptime history or a load test.\nThe candidate catalog is checked against a vendored official schema, including negative cases. The smoke host rejects wrong models, missing usage, incomplete streams and oversized responses. It writes timestamps, model identity, timing, token counts and pass/fail, not customer prompts or generated content.\n\n## Qualification plan\nOpenRouter synchronous traffic uses available capacity with early HTTP 429 and Retry-After when saturated, rather than an unbounded queue. Slow processing keeps SSE connections alive; keepalive comments are not generated tokens and must not count toward TTFT. Long queued work belongs to an explicitly asynchronous product contract with a deadline, cancellation and a price limit.\nRun scripts/api-smoke.cljs --provider-sample every 15 minutes, making one non-streaming and one streaming request sequentially, with a 45-second timeout. Append results to ~/.murakumo/openrouter-b70-samples.jsonl. Report with --provider-report.\nOur internal low-volume observation gate is seven days, at least 1,000 non-429 requests (at least 500 in each mode), at least 99.5% correctness success excluding HTTP 429, no observation gap over 45 minutes, latency percentiles retained as observations rather than pass/fail limits. Policy slow-cheap-v2 supersedes the previous internal 10-second completion / 5-second TTFT gate at the owner's direction; historical samples remain unchanged. The 45-second tiny-probe deadline still rejects unfinished probes and is not a universal product SLA. HTTP 429 is reported separately, never erased. These are internal targets, not OpenRouter's published acceptance criteria. A rolling window permits recovery from old failures; sleep or a stopped scheduler creates a coverage gap.\nEven when this observation gate passes, production-ready remains false. A small repeated prompt cannot establish long-context quality, parallel-load capacity, billing correctness or outage recovery.\n\n## Remaining submission gates\n1. Artifact identity and published license declarations VERIFIED on 2026-09-08: live SHA-256 matches the pinned lmstudio-community GGUF; Q4_K_M and Apache-2.0 source/card declarations checked. Conversion reproduction and exact quantizer source-commit attestation remain unverified. The public 16,384-token budget and one-token-over-limit rejection passed. See business-openrouter-b70-qualification-evidence-20260908.\n2. One long-context boundary request and one two-request concurrent pair passed on 2026-09-08. Sustained load, cancellation, queue overflow, 429/Retry-After and backend restart recovery remain open. Reserve capacity so existing workloads cannot consume the promised offer. Establish supported request parameters with measured results.\n3. Establish the OpenRouter credential, authorization and per-provider accounting boundary. Current anonymous public smoke success does not prove authenticated partner isolation or billable metering.\n4. Publish approved, final privacy/retention and terms pages covering edge, inference origin, asynchronous storage and operational logs. Current public legal pages are marked DRAFT; no whole-service ZDR claim is justified. Public URLs: https://murakumo.cloud/legal-privacy and https://murakumo.cloud/legal-terms.\n5. Confirm the contracting entity, billing contact, and either automatic top-up or invoicing with reconciliation. OpenRouter provider documentation allows either payment mechanism; monthly invoicing is not the only documented path. Do not infer legal or invoice readiness from a company name on a website.\n6. Finish seven-day observation and attach the raw ledger hash, UTC interval, request counts, 429 count, failure causes, throughput and latency percentiles. Cross-check public catalog against actual deployment.\n7. Verify candidate-specific cost per successful useful output, including retries, idle capacity, electricity, hardware amortization, operator payments and edge/storage fees. Do not reuse the older sparse-model cost estimate as proof for the dense 27B candidate.\n8. Rewrite the final submission from verified evidence, then submit and retain an external receipt. A draft, merged PR, deployed endpoint or scheduler is not an application receipt.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://openrouter.ai/providers/apply\nhttps://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/community/for-providers\nhttps://openrouter.ai/docs/assets/provider-monitor-schema-v2.openapi.json\nPrevious original-model plan: adr-2609011842-awai-japanese-models-openrouter-provider-application.\n"}]
